# Important Findings

* Knowledge Planner defaults to gpt-4o, because models.knowledgePlanner falls back to models.main. That is probably too expensive for a JSON planning step.

* QA also defaults to gpt-4o, because models.qa falls back to main. This may be unnecessary for run diagnosis.

* Alert Agent has AI settings in the main settings screen (ai.alert) but no main model select. Its actual model is either subagents.alert.model or config.models.main. This is a settings/runtime mismatch.

* Reranker has max output tokens configurable, but input size is hardcoded: each candidate sends up to 1800 chars, multiplied by up to 40 candidates before topK. This is likely a major hidden input-token cost.

* QA Trend ignores the normal QA temperature/token/timeout settings and hardcodes temperature: 0.1, maxTokens: 3000.

* chatCompletion() currently returns only text. It discards OpenRouter id, usage, token counts, and cost. That blocks the future hidden calibration agent from calculating exact per-step cost.

* OpenRouter already supports what we need later: non-streaming responses include usage data with token counts and optional cost, and generation stats can also be queried later by generation id via /api/v1/generation OpenRouter API reference. Stored prompt/completion content is available only if Input & Output Logging is enabled, currently beta/admin-controlled OpenRouter logging docs.

# Future Hidden Calibration Agent Requirement
The agent you described should be backend/admin-only, not customer-facing. It should collect per model call:
runId, messageId, step, model, prompt key/hash, input size, output size, OpenRouter generation id, prompt tokens, completion tokens, total cost, latency, response preview, quality verdict, and suggested cheaper equivalent model.
The natural integration point is src/openrouter.js, but we should not change it yet. First we need Phase 2: cost/token diagnosis from actual runs.

